WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.173
Welcome to Module Three: Evaluation and Tuning.

2
00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:06.311
How do you measure a system with no single right answer?

3
00:00:07.111 --> 00:00:11.349
Picture an insurer's AI assistant that answers questions about claims.

4
00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:14.488
In every demo it felt great, so the team shipped it.

5
00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:17.649
Then someone tweaked the prompt to sound friendlier.

6
00:00:17.979 --> 00:00:21.129
Nobody noticed that wrong coverage answers quietly doubled.

7
00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:24.076
The team had no metrics, just a feeling.

8
00:00:24.406 --> 00:00:27.961
And one end-to-end score would only say something broke, never where.

9
00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:31.634
So they added an AI judge to grade the answers.

10
00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:35.093
It loved long, confident replies, even the wrong ones.

11
00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:39.256
Next, they planned to fine-tune the model on policy documents.

12
00:00:39.586 --> 00:00:43.867
But policies change every quarter, and fine-tuning bakes facts into the weights.

13
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:48.990
Instead, they tested each piece alone, starting with retrieval, the search step.

14
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.150
The right policy clause was never being fetched at all.

15
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:56.079
They also checked trajectories, the path the agent took.

16
00:00:56.409 --> 00:01:00.689
Some right answers came after six redundant tool calls and a lucky guess.

17
00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.450
They rebuilt the judge to compare answers in pairs, with a clear rubric.

18
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.229
Then they checked it against human experts before trusting its scores.

19
00:01:10.029 --> 00:01:12.944
The real fix was better retrieval, not fine-tuning.

20
00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:17.491
And a golden test set now runs on every change, so no tweak slips through.

21
00:01:18.291 --> 00:01:22.998
Lesson one shows what to measure: components, steps, trajectories, and outcomes.

22
00:01:23.328 --> 00:01:27.587
Plus how to test on real traffic safely, with shadow and canary rollouts.

23
00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:31.665
Agents vary from run to run, so one run proves nothing.

24
00:01:31.995 --> 00:01:37.193
Passing once in k tries shows capability; passing every time shows reliability.

25
00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:42.381
Lesson two covers LLM-as-a-judge, and when a simple code check beats it.

26
00:01:42.711 --> 00:01:44.844
You'll name its four biases: position,

27
00:01:44.894 --> 00:01:47.717
verbosity, self-preference, and score compression.

28
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:53.544
Lesson three is the exam's most common decision: prompting, RAG, or fine-tuning.

29
00:01:53.874 --> 00:01:58.475
RAG looks facts up, fine-tuning shapes behaviour, and prompting covers the rest.

30
00:01:59.275 --> 00:02:01.785
You'll compare methods like LoRA and distillation.

31
00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:07.078
Then NVIDIA's data flywheel: curate, customise, evaluate, deploy, repeat.

32
00:02:07.878 --> 00:02:10.381
This domain is thirteen percent of the exam,

33
00:02:10.431 --> 00:02:13.631
and it rewards finding which layer a symptom belongs to.

34
00:02:13.961 --> 00:02:17.665
Measure first, fix the right layer, and that's where lesson one begins.
